---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Aside from '../../../components/Aside.astro';
import {i18nSidebar} from '../../../data/i18n-sidebar';
import {Code} from 'astro:components';
---

<DocsLayout title="Catalogs & Codegen" sidebar={i18nSidebar}
            description="YAML catalogs, the Gradle plugin, generated accessors and the build-time checker">

    <h1>Catalogs &amp; Codegen</h1>

    <p class="text-xl text-gray-400 !mt-2 !mb-8">
        YAML in, typed Kotlin out &mdash; and a checker that fails the build before your users find the gap.
    </p>

    <h2>Where catalogs live</h2>

    <p>One YAML file per locale, under the source set that owns them:</p>

    <Code code={`src/commonMain/i18n/messages.en.yaml
src/commonMain/i18n/messages.de.yaml
src/commonMain/i18n/messages.de-CH.yaml`} lang="text" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>Keys nest freely; nesting becomes a dotted key. Reserved Kotlin words and hyphenated keys are handled
        by the emitter, so you can name things naturally.</p>

    <Code code={`checkout:
  greeting: "Hello {{name}}!"
  is: "reserved words are fine"
  greet-user: "Hi {{first-name}}"
  items_one: "{{count}} item"
  items_other: "{{count}} items"
  nested:
    deep: "Deep {{value}}"`} lang="yaml" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <h2>Wiring the plugin</h2>

    <Code code={`apply<I18nPlugin>()

configure<I18nExtension> {
    packageName.set("com.example.app.i18n")
    moduleName.set("App")          // prefix for generated names; defaults to the project name
    fallbackLang.set("en")         // defines the generated API surface
    requiredLangs("de")            // must be at full key parity with the fallback
    // sourceSet.set("main")       // for a plain kotlin("jvm") module; default is commonMain
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>Generated code lands in <code>build/generated/i18n/&lt;sourceSet&gt;/kotlin</code> and is registered
        on the source set through the task provider, so every compilation depends on generation
        automatically. There is no manual wiring and no committed generated code.</p>

    <h2>What gets generated</h2>

    <p>Two things: a baked catalog object holding every locale's strings, and typed accessors hanging off
        the open translation surface.</p>

    <Code code={`// Generated — accessors named after your namespaces
val I18nTranslate.checkout: CheckoutI18n get() = CheckoutI18n(i18n)

// Usage — placeholders are named parameters
i18n.translate.checkout.greeting(name = "Alice")
i18n.translate.checkout.items(count = 3)
i18n.translate.checkout.nested.deep(value = "x")`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p><strong>The fallback catalog alone defines the API.</strong> Other languages may omit keys &mdash;
        they fall through the chain &mdash; but they can never introduce one the fallback does not have. That
        is what keeps the generated surface stable and makes a removed key a compile error at every call
        site.</p>

    <h2>The checker</h2>

    <p><code>checkI18n</code> is wired into <code>check</code>, so it runs with your tests. It compares every
        non-fallback catalog against the fallback:</p>

    <div class="not-prose my-8">
        <table class="w-full text-sm text-left">
            <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                <th class="py-2 pr-4 text-white font-semibold">Finding</th>
                <th class="py-2 pr-4 text-white font-semibold">Severity</th>
            </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-gray-400">
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5">
                <td class="py-2 pr-4">A <code>requiredLangs</code> language is missing a key, or absent entirely</td>
                <td class="py-2 pr-4">Error &mdash; fails the build</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5">
                <td class="py-2 pr-4">A locale <strong>introduces</strong> a placeholder the fallback lacks</td>
                <td class="py-2 pr-4">Error &mdash; fails the build</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5">
                <td class="py-2 pr-4">A locale defines a key the fallback does not have</td>
                <td class="py-2 pr-4">Warning &mdash; error under <code>-Pi18n.strict</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5">
                <td class="py-2 pr-4">An optional language is incomplete</td>
                <td class="py-2 pr-4">Warning &mdash; error under <code>-Pi18n.strict</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5">
                <td class="py-2 pr-4">A locale <strong>omits</strong> a placeholder the fallback has</td>
                <td class="py-2 pr-4">Info &mdash; logged only, never fails</td>
            </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <p>A report is written to <code>build/reports/i18n/check.txt</code>. Note that
        <code>requiredLangs</code> takes base languages only &mdash; a region inherits from its base, so
        requiring <code>de-CH</code> is rejected outright rather than silently doing nothing.</p>

    <Aside>Note the asymmetry, which is easy to get backwards: <em>introducing</em> an unknown placeholder
        is an error, because it can never be filled. <em>Omitting</em> one is only INFO &mdash; a translator
        dropping <code>{'{{count}}'}</code> still produces a grammatical sentence, just one missing a number,
        and there are languages where leaving it out is the correct translation.
    </Aside>

    <h2>One catalog per module</h2>

    <p>Each module generates and ships its own catalog. An application installs them in order, and
        <strong>later installs win</strong> on shared keys:</p>

    <Code code={`val i18n = I18n(locale, fallback) {
    install(KraftFormsI18nCatalog)   // framework first
    install(AppI18nCatalog)          // app last — overrides on conflict
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>So an app can restate a framework string without forking the framework, and a library can ship
        sensible defaults without dictating them.</p>

    <div class="not-prose mt-8">
        <a href="/ultra/i18n/locales"
           class="inline-flex items-center px-6 py-3 bg-brand-600 text-white font-semibold rounded-lg hover:bg-brand-500 transition-colors">
            Locales &amp; resolution &rarr;
        </a>
    </div>

</DocsLayout>
